{0 Server}

The package [silica.server] ({!Silica_server}) has the server side of an app:
it renders pages, answers Remote calls, runs Live sockets, keeps sessions,
checks CSRF tokens and origins, serves the hashed assets and adds the security
headers. It does not open a port and does not depend on an HTTP library.

{1:adapter The server and the adapter}

[Silica_server.Make (Io)] takes the concurrency of the HTTP library: a task
type with [return], [bind], [sleep], [async], a clock and a promise. An
adapter connects the result to one HTTP library. It only translates: requests,
whole WebSocket messages and files in, responses out. The repository has one
adapter, over [tiny_httpd], in [example/server_adapter/]. {!page-recipes.hosting}
shows a complete server with it.

[Server.create config ~read ~handlers ~islands ~topics ~page ()] makes the
server:

- [config]: the policy. {!Silica_server.default_config} needs the allowed
  origins and a secret of at least 16 bytes for the CSRF tokens.
- [assets] and [read]: the files of the client build (see
  {{!section-assets}Assets}).
- [handlers]: the Remote endpoints.
- [islands] and [topics]: the Live components and topics.
- [page]: the function that renders a page for a [GET].

[Server.handle server request] answers one HTTP request.
[Server.upgrade server request] answers a WebSocket request. The adapter calls
[Server.sweep] about every 5 seconds.

{1:try Try the policy}

This module makes a server with a synchronous [Io], so that a test or this
page can call [handle] directly. [visit] loads the page and keeps the session
cookie and the CSRF token that the page has. [call] sends a Remote call with
them:

{@ocaml file=examples/testing/doc_server.ml[
module Io = struct
  type 'a t = 'a

  let return value = value
  let bind value next = next value
  let sleep _ = ()
  let tasks : (unit -> unit) Queue.t = Queue.create ()
  let async task = Queue.add task tasks
  let now () = 0.

  type 'a promise = 'a option ref

  let promise () = ref None
  let resolve promise value = if !promise = None then promise := Some value

  let await promise ~timeout:_ =
    while not (Queue.is_empty tasks) do
      (Queue.take tasks) ()
    done;
    !promise
end

module Server = Silica_server.Make (Io)

let handlers =
  [
    Server.implement Tutorial_api.greeting (fun _context name ->
        Ok ("Hello, " ^ name)
    );
  ]

let config =
  Silica_server.default_config
    ~origins:(Silica_server.Origins [ "https://app.example" ])
    ~secret:"a secret of more than 16 bytes"

let server =
  Server.create config
    ~read:(fun _ -> None)
    ~handlers ~islands:[] ~topics:[]
    ~page:(fun (page : Server.page) ->
      Server.render_page_settled
        (fun _url -> Doc_hello.component)
        ~status:(fun _ -> 200)
        ~scripts:[] ~csrf:page.csrf ~nonce:page.nonce ~handlers
        ~context:page.context page.url
    )
    ()

let request ?(headers = []) ?(body = "") meth path =
  { Silica_server.meth; path; query = []; headers; body }

let after marker text =
  let length = String.length marker in
  let rec find i =
    if String.sub text i length = marker then
      i + length
    else
      find (i + 1)
  in
  let start = find 0 in
  String.sub text start (String.index_from text start '"' - start)

let visit () =
  let page = Server.handle server (request "GET" "/") in
  let cookie =
    List.hd (String.split_on_char ';' (List.assoc "set-cookie" page.headers))
  in
  (cookie, after "name=\"silica-csrf\" content=\"" page.body)

let call ?(origin = "https://app.example") ?token path body =
  let cookie, page_token = visit () in
  Server.handle server
    (request "POST" path ~body
       ~headers:
         [
           ("cookie", cookie);
           ("origin", origin);
           ("content-type", "application/json");
           ("x-silica-csrf", Option.value token ~default:page_token);
         ]
    )
]}

{1:requests The HTTP policy}

[handle] answers a request as follows:

- [GET /_silica/health] answers 200 [ok].
- A [GET] under [/static/] serves a file of the client build.
- A path of a handler must be [POST]; another method gets 405.
- Any other [GET] is a page. It finds the session by its cookie, or makes a
  new session and sets the cookie. The answer has
  [cache-control: private, no-store].

{@ocaml[
# let health = Doc_server.Server.handle Doc_server.server
    (Doc_server.request "GET" "/_silica/health");;
val health : Silica_server.response =
  {Silica_server.status = 200;
   headers =
    [("referrer-policy", "same-origin");
     ("x-content-type-options", "nosniff"); ("cache-control", "no-store");
     ("content-type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8")];
   body = "ok"}
]}

Every request that is not a [GET] must pass these checks, in this order:

+ The body is at most [max_body] bytes, else 413.
+ [sec-fetch-site], when the browser sends it, is [same-origin] or [none],
  else 403.
+ [origin] is present and allowed, else 403.
+ The cookie names a live session, else 403.
+ [content-type] starts with [application/json], else 415.
+ [x-silica-csrf] is the token of the session, else 403.

{@ocaml[
# (Doc_server.call "/api/greeting" {|"Ada"|}).body;;
- : string = "\"Hello, Ada\""
# (Doc_server.call ~origin:"https://other.example" "/api/greeting" {|"Ada"|}).status;;
- : int = 403
# (Doc_server.call ~token:"not the token" "/api/greeting" {|"Ada"|}).status;;
- : int = 403
]}

Every answer has [x-content-type-options: nosniff] and
[referrer-policy: same-origin]. An HTML page also has a
[content-security-policy] with a nonce for this answer: pass [page.nonce] to
the render.

{1:origins Origins}

[Origins list] gives the allowed values of the [Origin] header, matched in
full, such as ["https://app.example.com"]. [Localhost] is for development
only: the origin must be the host of the request, and that host must be
[localhost], [127.0.0.1] or [[::1]].

{1:sessions Sessions and CSRF}

A session is temporary server data for one visitor, found by the cookie
[silica_sid]. The cookie is [HttpOnly], [SameSite=Lax] and [Path=/], and
[Secure] when [secure_cookie] is set. With the default store, a session lives
8 hours when unused and 7 days at most, and the store keeps at most 10 000
sessions. Sessions live in memory: a restart ends them. [config.sessions]
takes another store.

The CSRF token is tied to the session. The page gets it as [page.csrf] and
writes it in a [meta] element of the head; every Remote call and every Live
hello carries it. So a page of another site cannot act for the visitor.

When a Live socket names a session that the server no longer knows, for
example after a restart, the server accepts the upgrade and closes the socket
at once with {!Silica.Protocol.close_session}. A browser cannot read the
status of a refused upgrade. The client then reloads the page once, which
gives it a new session and token.

{1:remote Remote handlers}

[Server.implement endpoint f] answers an endpoint. [f] gets the context (the
request and the session) and the decoded input, and gives
[Ok output] or [Error message] in [Io]. An input that does not decode gets
400. An [Error], or an exception that the handler raises while it runs, gets
500 with the fixed body ["internal error"]; the detail goes to the log, not to
the client.

{1:render Pages}

The [page] function gets a [page] record: the context, the URL, the CSRF token,
the nonce and [asset], which gives the URL of a file of the client build. It
renders the app with one of these:

- [Server.render_page]: mount the root at the URL, start the machines and
  print the view.
- [Server.render_page_settled]: also run the Remote calls of the first render
  against the handlers, step the machines with the answers, and repeat until
  no call is left. It writes the answers into the page, so the browser does not
  call again. See {!page-hydration}.
- [Server.render_component]: a component with no URL.

[status] gives the HTTP status from the root snapshot. The last
[Set_status], [Set_title] and [Set_document_attribute] of the render change the
status, the title and the attributes of [<html>]. A [Navigate] or [Replace]
makes the answer a redirect: status 303 with a [location] header and no body.
An exception that no boundary catches gives status 500 and an error page,
with the details only in [dev] mode.

{1:assets Assets}

{!Silica_server.Assets.of_metafile} reads the esbuild metafile: the map from
the name of a file before the hash, such as [client_main.js], to the file with
the hash. The page asks for a file by its name with [page.asset]. [read]
gives the bytes of a file under [/static/]; the server never asks for a name
with [..], a leading slash or control characters.

A file with a hash in its name gets
[cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable]. Other files get
[no-cache] and an [etag], and a matching [if-none-match] gets 304.

{1:live Live sockets}

The browser opens one WebSocket on [/_silica/live] for each island and each
topic subscription. [Server.upgrade] refuses the request when the origin is
missing or not allowed. The first message must be a hello with the protocol
version, the CSRF token of the session, and an island or a topic that the
server knows; otherwise the server closes the socket with a code of
{!Silica.Protocol}. Settings in [config]:

- [heartbeat]: [sweep] closes a socket with no traffic for two heartbeats
  (default 30 seconds).
- [live_keep]: the state of an island or a topic stays this long after its
  last socket closed (default 30 minutes). An island that comes back in time
  gets its state again.
- [max_message]: a larger message from the client is dropped, and the client
  gets an error message (default 64 KiB).

{2:islands Islands}

An island is a Live component ({!Silica.Live.t}) whose machine runs on the
server, one instance for each connection. Give it to the server in
[islands], as [Server.Island live]. The server runs its Remote calls against
the handlers directly and its timers with [Io.sleep]. See
{!page-hydration.islands}.

{2:publish Topics and publish}

A topic is declared in shared code. The server lists it in [topics] with an
[init] function, which gives the first state of a key that nobody published
yet. A handler publishes the new full state of a key with [Server.publish];
each subscriber gets it with a sequence number one higher than the last. The
example app publishes the votes after each vote:

From [example/server/main.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
  let vote _ () =
    let n = Atomic.fetch_and_add votes 1 + 1 in
    ignore
      (S.publish (Option.get !server) Example_model.votes_topic ~key:"main" n);
    Ok n
  in
]}

From [example/server/main.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
      ~topics:
        [
          S.Topic_def
            {
              topic = Example_model.votes_topic;
              init = (fun _ ~key:_ -> Ok (Atomic.get votes));
            };
        ]
]}

The [observer] of the config hears the log lines, each request with its status
and time, and each socket that opens or closes.
